Running the GPU version on Palmetto
===================================

What runs: in.dpd26, the reference script in.dpd with only the two changes
that this newer LAMMPS requires: 'neighbor 0.3 bin' (was 'multi') and no
'comm_modify mode multi' lines. All cutoffs are 1.0, so the physics and the
protocol (1000 + 5000 + 1000 steps, fix print 1 to com.dat) are unchanged.
Each job runs one box on one GPU; submit as many jobs as you want runs.
GPU runs are not bit-identical to the CPU code (different summation order),
so compare averages over several runs with the reference results.

1. Get the code, in a directory that compute nodes can see
----------------------------------------------------------

    git clone https://github.com/ryan1w/Oil-Drop-Surfactant-on-GPU.git
    cd Oil-Drop-Surfactant-on-GPU

2. Copy the 8x and 16x data files (not in Git)
----------------------------------------------

From the desktop, through the data transfer node (login nodes are only for
small files):

    scp data_sim_8x.in data_sim_16x.in USER@hpcdtn01.rcd.clemson.edu:/path/to/Oil-Drop-Surfactant-on-GPU/

Then, on Palmetto, in the repository directory (all three lines must say OK):

    sha256sum -c cluster/palmetto/data.sha256

3. Check the module names once
------------------------------

    module avail gcc/12.3.0 cuda/12.3.0 openmpi/5.0.1 cmake

If a name differs, edit cluster/palmetto/setup.sh, the only place they appear.

4. Submit, from the repository directory
----------------------------------------

    sbatch cluster/palmetto/run.sbatch 4x
    sbatch cluster/palmetto/run.sbatch 8x
    sbatch cluster/palmetto/run.sbatch 16x

Submit the same line again for more runs of a box. The default GPU is an
A100; for another type, add for example --gpus=h100:1 (or v100, l40s, a40):

    sbatch --gpus=h100:1 cluster/palmetto/run.sbatch 16x

The first job on a GPU type also builds LAMMPS from lammps26/ (20-40 min,
into build/<ARCH>/); jobs that start meanwhile wait for that build and then
use it. Later jobs start the simulation right away.

5. Results
----------

Each job writes runs/<box>_<jobid>/ with log.lammps and com.dat (the same
files as the reference runs) and run_info.txt (GPU, executable, checksums).
The job log dpd-<jobid>.out ends with a summary: time per step of each of the
three runs, dangerous builds and warnings.

Notes
-----

- A few "Dangerous builds" right after the time step changes from 0.001 to
  0.005 (mostly in 16x) are expected. The reference script does the same;
  the data files start the fluid at random positions.
- If your account needs a partition or account name, add it to the sbatch
  line (-p ..., -A ...) or set it once: export SBATCH_PARTITION=... /
  export SBATCH_ACCOUNT=...
- If srun cannot start the program (an MPI or PMIx error before LAMMPS
  prints anything), replace 'srun' by 'mpirun -np 1' in run.sbatch.
- A failed build leaves its messages in build/<ARCH>-cmake.log and
  build/<ARCH>-build.log; the next job tries again.
- run.sbatch asks for 8 cores, 16 GB and 2 h so that it can also do the
  build; a run alone needs a few minutes.

Palmetto documentation used: GPU requests and partitions
https://docs.rcd.clemson.edu/palmetto/job_management/submit/ , modules
https://docs.rcd.clemson.edu/software/modules/ , file transfer
https://docs.rcd.clemson.edu/palmetto/transfer/overview/
